(Efficacy Comparison of IL-23 Inhibition and PDE-4 Inhibition in Systemic Psoriasis) Tj
T*
(Treatment) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Three hundred fifty-two adults with moderate plaque psoriasis were randomized to receive) Tj
T*
(either subcutaneous IL-23 inhibitor \(n = 118\) or oral PDE-4 inhibitor \(n = 234\). At 16) Tj
T*
(weeks, the IL-23 inhibitor group achieved significantly higher rates of 90% or greater) Tj
T*
(skin clearance \(56.8% vs 5.3%, p < 0.001\) and physician-assessed complete/nearly-complete) Tj
T*
(clearing with marked improvement \(75.4% vs 18.8%, p < 0.001\). Among the oral agent group,) Tj
T*
(participants not achieving treatment response threshold were re-randomized at week 16 to) Tj
T*
(either switch to IL-23 inhibitor or continue their current therapy. At 52 weeks, those who) Tj
T*
(switched to IL-23 inhibitor achieved 90% clearance in 72.2% of cases compared to only 2.6%) Tj
T*
(who continued the original oral agent. The IL-23 inhibitor was well-tolerated; most) Tj
T*
(frequent adverse events were upper respiratory tract infections and COVID-19. The oral) Tj
T*
(agent group experienced higher rates of gastrointestinal side effects. Most participants) Tj
T*
(in both groups reported improvement in psoriasis-related quality of life and reduced) Tj
T*
(disease-related burden. These results demonstrate substantially superior efficacy of IL-23) Tj
T*
(inhibition compared with oral PDE-4 inhibition, particularly when given as initial) Tj
T*
(therapy.) Tj
ET
